Begin Exploring Related Columns
Below is a “thinking sheet” that identifies the tables / columns that matter, the key filtering conditions, and then ten exploratory SQL statements (simple → complex → close‑to‑final) you could run in SQLite to convince yourself the logic is sound before writing the single final‐answer query.

Relevant tables / columns and how they will be used
• olist_orders  
  – order_id : join key to other tables  
  – customer_id : to reach the customer’s unique id  
  – order_status : keep only 'delivered'  

• olist_customers  
  – customer_id : join to olist_orders  
  – customer_unique_id : needed for “distinct customer count”  

• olist_order_items  
  – order_id : join to olist_orders  
  – seller_id : the subject of every ranking  
  – price : for revenue  
  – freight_value : to compute profit = price – freight_value  

• olist_order_reviews  
  – order_id : join to olist_orders  
  – review_score : count 5‑star reviews  

Filtering conditions that will be used later
1. olist_orders.order_status = 'delivered'   -- only delivered orders  
2. olist_order_reviews.review_score = 5     -- for the 5‑star metric  

Ten step‑by‑step inspection queries
(Every query is limited to 20 rows; you can drop the LIMIT when you really run them.)

```sql
-- 1. View a handful of delivered orders
SELECT DISTINCT order_id, customer_id
FROM olist_orders
WHERE order_status = 'delivered'
LIMIT 20;

-- 2. Map delivered orders to their sellers (raw view)
SELECT DISTINCT oi.order_id, oi.seller_id
FROM olist_order_items AS oi
JOIN olist_orders       AS o  USING (order_id)
WHERE o.order_status = 'delivered'
LIMIT 20;

-- 3. Bring in the customer_unique_id for each seller/order combo
SELECT DISTINCT oi.seller_id,
       c.customer_unique_id
FROM olist_order_items AS oi
JOIN olist_orders       AS o  USING (order_id)
JOIN olist_customers    AS c  ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id
WHERE o.order_status = 'delivered'
LIMIT 20;

-- 4. Count distinct customer_unique_id per seller (first 20)
SELECT oi.seller_id,
       COUNT(DISTINCT c.customer_unique_id) AS distinct_customers
FROM olist_order_items AS oi
JOIN olist_orders    AS o  USING (order_id)
JOIN olist_customers AS c  ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id
WHERE o.order_status = 'delivered'
GROUP BY oi.seller_id
ORDER BY distinct_customers DESC
LIMIT 20;

-- 5. Preview profit per line‑item for delivered orders
SELECT oi.order_id,
       oi.seller_id,
       (oi.price - oi.freight_value) AS profit
FROM olist_order_items AS oi
JOIN olist_orders AS o USING (order_id)
WHERE o.order_status = 'delivered'
LIMIT 20;

-- 6. Aggregate profit per seller (top 20)
SELECT oi.seller_id,
       SUM(oi.price - oi.freight_value) AS total_profit
FROM olist_order_items AS oi
JOIN olist_orders AS o USING (order_id)
WHERE o.order_status = 'delivered'
GROUP BY oi.seller_id
ORDER BY total_profit DESC
LIMIT 20;

-- 7. Count distinct delivered orders per seller (top 20)
SELECT oi.seller_id,
       COUNT(DISTINCT oi.order_id) AS delivered_orders
FROM olist_order_items AS oi
JOIN olist_orders AS o USING (order_id)
WHERE o.order_status = 'delivered'
GROUP BY oi.seller_id
ORDER BY delivered_orders DESC
LIMIT 20;

-- 8. Pull all 5‑star reviews tied to delivered orders
SELECT DISTINCT oi.seller_id,
       r.order_id
FROM olist_order_reviews AS r
JOIN olist_orders        AS o  USING (order_id)
JOIN olist_order_items   AS oi USING (order_id)
WHERE o.order_status = 'delivered'
  AND r.review_score = 5
LIMIT 20;

-- 9. Count 5‑star reviews per seller (top 20)
SELECT oi.seller_id,
       COUNT(*) AS five_star_reviews
FROM olist_order_reviews AS r
JOIN olist_orders       AS o  USING (order_id)
JOIN olist_order_items  AS oi USING (order_id)
WHERE o.order_status = 'delivered'
  AND r.review_score = 5
GROUP BY oi.seller_id
ORDER BY five_star_reviews DESC
LIMIT 20;

-- 10. (Near‑final) Union the four “winners” into one labeled result set
SELECT 'Most distinct customers' AS achievement,
       seller_id,
       distinct_customers AS value
FROM (
      SELECT oi.seller_id,
             COUNT(DISTINCT c.customer_unique_id) AS distinct_customers
      FROM olist_order_items AS oi
      JOIN olist_orders    AS o  USING (order_id)
      JOIN olist_customers AS c  ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id
      WHERE o.order_status = 'delivered'
      GROUP BY oi.seller_id
      ORDER BY distinct_customers DESC
      LIMIT 1
)
UNION ALL
SELECT 'Highest profit',
       seller_id,
       total_profit
FROM (
      SELECT oi.seller_id,
             SUM(oi.price - oi.freight_value) AS total_profit
      FROM olist_order_items AS oi
      JOIN olist_orders AS o USING (order_id)
      WHERE o.order_status = 'delivered'
      GROUP BY oi.seller_id
      ORDER BY total_profit DESC
      LIMIT 1
)
UNION ALL
SELECT 'Most delivered orders',
       seller_id,
       delivered_orders
FROM (
      SELECT oi.seller_id,
             COUNT(DISTINCT oi.order_id) AS delivered_orders
      FROM olist_order_items AS oi
      JOIN olist_orders AS o USING (order_id)
      WHERE o.order_status = 'delivered'
      GROUP BY oi.seller_id
      ORDER BY delivered_orders DESC
      LIMIT 1
)
UNION ALL
SELECT 'Most 5‑star reviews',
       seller_id,
       five_star_reviews
FROM (
      SELECT oi.seller_id,
             COUNT(*) AS five_star_reviews
      FROM olist_order_reviews AS r
      JOIN olist_orders      AS o  USING (order_id)
      JOIN olist_order_items AS oi USING (order_id)
      WHERE o.order_status = 'delivered'
        AND r.review_score = 5
      GROUP BY oi.seller_id
      ORDER BY five_star_reviews DESC
      LIMIT 1
)
LIMIT 20;
```

Run the earlier simpler queries first if you want to inspect intermediate joins, then the last query (#10) will give the single, consolidated answer requested (one row per “achievement,” each with the winning seller_id and its metric).
